---

<section class="section adapt" id="adaptation" aria-labelledby="adapt-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>Built to keep adapting</p>

    <div class="grid adapt__top">
      <div class="adapt__copy">
        <h2 class="h2" id="adapt-title" data-reveal style="--i:1">
          The future of work is not a one-time redesign problem. <em>It is an ongoing adaptation problem.</em>
        </h2>
        <p class="lede adapt__lede" data-reveal style="--i:2">
          As AI capabilities evolve, workflows, roles, skills, decision rights, governance, and rewards need to
          evolve with them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="adapt__stat" data-reveal style="--i:2">
        <svg class="adapt__loop" viewBox="0 0 120 120" aria-hidden="true">
          <circle cx="60" cy="60" r="52" />
          <path d="M60 8 A52 52 0 1 1 15 34" />
          <path class="adapt__loop-head" d="M6.7 38.4 L15 34 L15.3 43.4" />
        </svg>
        <p class="adapt__num">2×</p>
        <p class="adapt__text">
          Gartner forecasts that by 2028, organizations that establish continuous work redesign as a core
          capability will be twice as likely to sustain AI-driven work transformation. Gartner describes this
          capability as the ongoing adaptation of workflows, decision rights, jobs, and talent deployment as AI
          capabilities and business priorities evolve.
        </p>
        <p class="cite adapt__cite">Gartner forecast, September 15, 2026.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="grid adapt__roles">
      <h3 class="h3 adapt__roles-title" data-reveal>Roles and rewards that support the redesigned work</h3>
      <p class="body adapt__roles-body" data-reveal style="--i:1">
        Where deeper redesign is required, I draw on my experience in workforce architecture to clarify how roles,
        skills, human judgment, decision rights, system access, governance, and rewards support the new workflow.
        The aim is to help people understand their contribution and give leaders a practical basis for adapting the
        work as AI capabilities evolve.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .adapt__top {
    margin-top: 28px;
    row-gap: 56px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .adapt__copy {
    grid-column: 1 / 8;
  }
  .adapt__lede {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  .adapt__stat {
    position: relative;
    grid-column: 9 / 13;
    padding: 36px 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }
  .adapt__loop {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: 36px;
    width: 76px;
    height: 76px;
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--teal);
    stroke-width: 1.2;
  }
  .adapt__loop circle {
    opacity: 0.18;
  }
  .adapt__num {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: clamp(88px, 10vw, 140px);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    color: var(--teal-deep);
  }
  .adapt__text {
    margin-top: 24px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .adapt__cite {
    margin-top: 16px;
  }
  .adapt__roles {
    margin-top: clamp(80px, 10vw, 136px);
    padding-top: 40px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    row-gap: 20px;
  }
  .adapt__roles-title {
    grid-column: 1 / 5;
  }
  .adapt__roles-body {
    grid-column: 6 / 12;
  }

  :global(.js) .adapt__loop path:first-of-type {
    stroke-dasharray: 330;
    stroke-dashoffset: 330;
    transition: stroke-dashoffset 1.6s cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1) 0.3s;
  }
  :global(.js) .adapt__stat.is-in .adapt__loop path:first-of-type {
    stroke-dashoffset: 0;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global(.js) .adapt__loop path:first-of-type {
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
